{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478654} {:answer/id #uuid "5f082dfc-3b85-5a93-bc09-af1e742628ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478693} {:answer/id #uuid "65dcc751-70ac-5835-895f-9afe1c6981be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478708} {:answer/id #uuid "9290ba2f-84e6-50ba-bd20-50955e2e64ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478796} {:answer/id #uuid "db8d4f50-3033-5b61-9824-d94717ad9840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65dcc751-70ac-5835-895f-9afe1c6981be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478708} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc384e98-6294-5707-b8e0-2ed99c0215ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d0c1517-fefb-518c-9505-969066e0bce6" :question/math-academy-id "q-110601" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:4x+5y-z=0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $4x+5y-z=0$ since $4\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ since ${0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. This subset has only one element, the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩,$ which is a special subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154b8691-57de-5b5e-86f8-c31acc6d30e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478587} {:answer/id #uuid "62d73fd1-aa7f-5ec7-8f6d-743894ccf613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478702} {:answer/id #uuid "759885ae-9ef9-5c93-ab20-c1fe824077c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478734} {:answer/id #uuid "c3271208-f908-5d5d-adf8-766e9fc2f767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478874} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e434bd-13dd-5cf1-bb32-448ac1fcc0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2e434bd-13dd-5cf1-bb32-448ac1fcc0c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478911} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c014ca5-93c9-54e8-95bc-87d740d016c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ada9b840-b530-5081-8048-c09da4df32f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-110606" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:3x+4y=1\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x+3y=0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ consists only of the vector $⟨0,0⟩$, which is a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $3x+4y=1$ since $3\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It also represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x+3y=0$ since $0+3\\cdot 0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd96b419-3ee9-5f06-bfb3-c37ca8e5de77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464540} {:answer/id #uuid "06d1f1f0-b376-5f15-8c04-89021b547573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd96b419-3ee9-5f06-bfb3-c37ca8e5de77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} 